{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After his father's death in 1152 Raymond II was succeeded by his son Raymond III who was still a minor at the time. *Early Life and Regency* Raymond's mother Hodierna acted as regent for her son until he came of age. In 1164 Raymond was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi during the Battle of Harim. He was released in 1173 after being held captive for nearly a decade.\n\nDuring his reign Raymond faced numerous challenges including the threat posed by the Zengid and Ayyubid dynasties. In 1174 Raymond allied himself with the Principality of Antioch and the Kingdom of Cilicia to counter the growing threat of the Zengids. *Military Campaigns and Alliances* Raymond's military campaigns were often marked by caution and pragmatism. In 1180 Raymond refused to support the invasion of Saladin's brother al-Adil in the Bekaa Valley. In 1183 Raymond opposed the marriage of Sibylla sister of Baldwin IV King of Jerusalem to Guy of Lusignan. \n\nRaymond's relationship with the King of Jerusalem was complex and often strained. In 1184 Raymond was appointed regent for Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. *Regency and Succession Crisis* As regent Raymond worked to secure the succession of Baldwin V the nephew of Baldwin IV. However after Baldwin IV's death in 1185 the succession crisis deepened. Raymond's rival Guy of Lusignan eventually became king in 1186. Raymond's refusal to recognize Guy as king led to a rift between the two men. In 1187 Raymond allied himself with Saladin to counter the threat posed by Guy of Lusignan. Raymond's actions were seen as treasonous by many of his contemporaries. He died later that year 